window.REVISION_SUBJECTS.push({"config":{"examBoard":"Edexcel International GCSE","subjectArea":"Religious Studies","subjectName":"Christianity","brandIcon":"✝️","pageTitle":"Christianity IGCSE Revision","brandTitle":"Christianity Revision","brandSubtitle":"Edexcel International GCSE","breadcrumb":"Religious Studies / Christianity","heroEyebrow":"Paper 1 · Paper 2 Christianity","papers":[{"id":"Paper 1","label":"Paper 1","title":"Beliefs and Values","subtitle":"modules 1.1–2.8"},{"id":"Paper 2","label":"Paper 2","title":"Religious Community","subtitle":"Christianity"}],"footerNote":"Questions are written to match the official Edexcel International GCSE Religious Studies specification. Model answers are original indicative exemplars, not official Edexcel mark schemes or past paper material. Accepted answers may vary.","storageId":"edexcel_christianity"},"questions":{"paper1":[{"id":"c-p1-1-1-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The universe and the place of human beings","question":"Choose one religion. Outline two teachings about the origins of the universe.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Genesis 1 teaches that God created the universe out of nothing (ex nihilo) in an ordered sequence of six days, speaking light, sky, land, plants, sun and moon, sea creatures, birds, land animals and finally human beings into existence, before resting on the seventh day.\n\n2. Genesis 2 gives a second account in which God forms the first man, Adam, from the dust of the ground and breathes life into him, and later forms Eve from Adam's rib, showing that human life is directly and personally given by God rather than arising by chance.","evidence":["Genesis 1:1–31 (six days of creation)","Genesis 2:7 (breath of life)","\"Let there be light\"","Ex nihilo creation"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"creation"},{"id":"c-p1-1-1-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The universe and the place of human beings","question":"“God caused the world to exist.”\nEvaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Genesis opens with the claim that \"In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth\", so for many Christians the Bible itself is direct testimony that God caused the world to exist.\n\nA second consideration is: The cosmological argument, associated with Thomas Aquinas, reasons that everything which exists has a cause, and that an infinite chain of causes is impossible, so there must be an uncaused first cause which people call God.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Scientific explanations such as the Big Bang theory and evolution by natural selection account for the origin and development of the universe and life through natural processes, without needing to appeal to a creator.\n\nA further consideration is: Non-religious thinkers argue that the existence of unnecessary suffering, and the lack of direct empirical evidence for God, make a divine cause of the world an unproven and unnecessary assumption.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: many Christians, including those who accept scientific accounts such as theistic evolution, would argue the two views are not opposites: God could be the ultimate cause working through the processes that science describes. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Genesis 1:1","Cosmological argument (Thomas Aquinas)","Big Bang theory","Theistic evolution"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"creation"},{"id":"c-p1-1-2-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Human nature and the human condition","question":"Choose one religion. Outline two beliefs about the immortality of the soul in this religion.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Most Christians believe that human beings are made in the image of God (imago Dei, Genesis 1:27) and are given an immortal soul which is distinct from the physical body and survives physical death.\n\n2. St Paul teaches in 1 Corinthians 15 that believers will ultimately receive a transformed, resurrected body, so the soul's immortality is linked to the Christian hope of bodily resurrection rather than the soul alone drifting on forever.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27 (imago Dei)","1 Corinthians 15:42–44 (resurrection body)","Immortality of the soul","Body and soul dualism"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"soul"},{"id":"c-p1-1-2-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Human nature and the human condition","question":"“Everyone has an immortal soul.”\nEvaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Christians generally teach that because human beings are made in the image of God, every person has a soul that is distinct from the body and that will continue to exist after physical death.\n\nA second consideration is: Genesis 2:7 describes God breathing the breath of life into Adam, which many Christians interpret as showing that human life and spiritual identity come directly from God and are not merely material.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Humanists and other non-religious thinkers argue that there is no scientific evidence for a soul separate from the brain; consciousness, personality and memory can all be explained as products of brain activity that end at death.\n\nA further consideration is: Some within Christianity itself, such as Christian physicalists, argue the Bible's emphasis on bodily resurrection (rather than a separate immortal soul) suggests the whole person, not a detachable soul, is what God values and recreates.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: the strength of someone's view often depends on whether they trust religious testimony and revelation, or require scientific/empirical evidence before accepting a claim about life beyond the body. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27 (imago Dei)","Genesis 2:7 (breath of life)","Christian physicalism","Materialist/Humanist view of consciousness"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"soul"},{"id":"c-p1-1-3-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Selfishness, greed, ignorance and sin","question":"Identify three examples of sin.","marks":3,"command":"Identify","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A clear three-point response could be:\n\n1. Breaking one of the Ten Commandments, such as stealing, murder or adultery (Exodus 20).\n2. Failing to love God and love one's neighbour, which Jesus taught were the two greatest commandments (Mark 12:30–31).\n3. Pride or self-centredness that puts one's own will above God's will, which many Christians see as the root of sin shown in Adam and Eve's disobedience in Genesis 3.","evidence":["Exodus 20 (Ten Commandments)","Mark 12:30–31 (Greatest Commandment)","Genesis 3 (the Fall)","Romans 3:23"],"examTip":"Give three distinct, relevant facts. One clear point earns each mark; development is not required for this question type.","answerTopic":"sin"},{"id":"c-p1-1-3-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Selfishness, greed, ignorance and sin","question":"Choose one religion. Explain what its followers believe about sin.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Most Christians trace the origin of sin to the Fall in Genesis 3, where Adam and Eve disobey God by eating the forbidden fruit; this is traditionally called Original Sin and is understood to have affected all of humanity, leaving people naturally inclined towards wrongdoing.\n\nSecond, Sin is often defined as \"missing the mark\" or falling short of what God intends; Romans 3:23 states that \"all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God\", meaning sin is seen as a universal human condition rather than something only a few people do.\n\nFinally, Christians believe sin damages a person's relationship with God, but that through Jesus' death and resurrection, forgiveness and reconciliation with God become possible; this is why confession, repentance and grace are central to Christian teaching about overcoming sin.","evidence":["Genesis 3 (the Fall, Original Sin)","Romans 3:23","Romans 6:23 (\"the wages of sin is death\")","Confession and repentance"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"sin"},{"id":"c-p1-1-4-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Free will, determinism and predestination","question":"Outline two religious beliefs about free will.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Many Christians believe God deliberately gave human beings free will so that love and obedience to God would be freely chosen rather than forced, as shown by Adam and Eve being able to choose whether or not to eat the forbidden fruit in Genesis 3.\n\n2. Free will is used in the free will defence, a response to the problem of evil which argues that moral evil exists because God allows humans to misuse their freedom, rather than evil being caused directly by God.","evidence":["Genesis 2–3 (the choice in Eden)","Free will defence","Augustine on the Fall","Moral responsibility"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"free-will"},{"id":"c-p1-1-4-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Free will, determinism and predestination","question":"“If God is omnipotent, then we have no free will.”\nEvaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Calvinist and other predestinarian Christians argue from passages such as Romans 8:29–30, which speaks of people being \"predestined\" and \"called\", that an all-powerful and all-knowing God has already determined who will be saved, which seems to leave little room for genuine free will.\n\nA second consideration is: If God's will is genuinely sovereign over everything that happens, some argue it is hard to see how a human choice could ever be truly independent of God's power and foreknowledge.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Most Christians, particularly in the Catholic and Arminian Protestant traditions, argue that God's omnipotence includes the power to freely give creatures real freedom; God's omniscience means God knows what people will choose, not that God forces the choice.\n\nA further consideration is: The free will defence relies on humans having real moral freedom, since it explains moral evil as the result of human choices rather than God's action; without genuine free will, humans could not fairly be held responsible for sin.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: compatibilist positions try to show that divine omnipotence/foreknowledge and human freedom can both be true at once, while others think the two genuinely conflict. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Romans 8:29–30 (predestination)","Free will defence","Arminianism vs Calvinism","Compatibilism"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"free-will"},{"id":"c-p1-1-5-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Ultimate reality","question":"Identify three characteristics of God.","marks":3,"command":"Identify","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A clear three-point response could be:\n\n1. Omnipotent: all-powerful, able to do anything that is logically possible.\n2. Omniscient and omnibenevolent: all-knowing and perfectly loving/good, as described throughout the Bible, for example 1 John 4:8, \"God is love\".\n3. Trinity: one God who exists eternally as three persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit.","evidence":["Omnipotence, omniscience, omnibenevolence","1 John 4:8 (\"God is love\")","The Trinity","Eternal and transcendent"],"examTip":"Give three distinct, relevant facts. One clear point earns each mark; development is not required for this question type.","answerTopic":"god"},{"id":"c-p1-1-5-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Ultimate reality","question":"Choose one religion. Explain why there are different attitudes to the nature and character of God in this religion.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Christianity teaches belief in one God who exists as a Trinity of three persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit; while all mainstream Christians accept this doctrine, different denominations emphasise different aspects of God's nature, for example the fatherhood of God or the closeness of the Holy Spirit.\n\nSecond, Catholic and Orthodox Christians often emphasise God's majesty and mystery, approaching God through sacraments, saints and the intercession of Mary, while many Protestant traditions emphasise a direct, personal relationship with God without such intermediaries.\n\nFinally, Christians also hold different views in response to suffering: classical theism emphasises God's unchanging power and goodness even amid suffering, while some modern theologians (process theology) suggest God's power is better understood as persuasive rather than all-controlling, which changes how God's character is understood.","evidence":["The Trinity (Father, Son, Holy Spirit)","Catholic vs Protestant emphases","Intercession of saints and Mary","Classical theism vs process theology"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"god"},{"id":"c-p1-1-6-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Belief, uncertainty and unbelief","question":"Outline two reasons why some people believe in the existence of God.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. The design argument, associated with William Paley's analogy of a watch implying a watchmaker, claims that the order, complexity and apparent purpose seen in nature suggest an intelligent designer rather than pure chance.\n\n2. Many people believe in God because of religious experience, such as a sense of God's presence in prayer or worship, which they interpret as personal evidence of God that cannot easily be explained away.","evidence":["William Paley's watchmaker analogy","Design/teleological argument","Religious experience","Revelation through the Bible"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"belief"},{"id":"c-p1-1-6-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"Belief, uncertainty and unbelief","question":"“There is no proof that God exists.”\nEvaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Philosopher Antony Flew argued that religious believers' claims about God are unfalsifiable: no amount of evidence seems able to count against belief in God, which suggests such belief is not making a testable, evidence-based claim at all.\n\nA second consideration is: The continued existence of unexplained suffering in the world is used by some as evidence against an all-powerful, all-loving God, since such a God would be expected to prevent needless suffering if proof of God's goodness were available.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Supporters of the design and cosmological arguments claim that the order of the universe and the fact that anything exists at all point strongly, even if not with mathematical certainty, towards a creator; for them, this counts as good evidence rather than proof.\n\nA further consideration is: Many Christians would respond that faith does not require scientific-style proof; religious experience and a personal relationship with God provide a different, but still valid, kind of justification for belief.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: whether \"proof\" has to mean scientific, repeatable evidence, or whether philosophical arguments and personal religious experience can also count as legitimate grounds for belief. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Antony Flew on falsification","Design/teleological argument","Cosmological argument","Religious experience"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"belief"},{"id":"c-p1-1-7-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The problem of evil and suffering","question":"Choose one religion. Outline two beliefs about the causes of suffering.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. The Augustinian theodicy traces suffering to the Fall in Genesis 3: when Adam and Eve disobeyed God, sin entered the world and corrupted a previously perfect creation, so suffering is a consequence of human wrongdoing rather than something God directly causes.\n\n2. The free will defence explains moral evil (suffering caused by human actions, such as cruelty or war) as the result of God giving humans genuine freedom; suffering happens because people sometimes misuse that freedom rather than because God wills it.","evidence":["Genesis 3 (the Fall)","Augustinian theodicy","Free will defence","Moral evil vs natural evil"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"suffering"},{"id":"c-p1-1-7-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The problem of evil and suffering","question":"“Everyone suffers; therefore God cannot exist.”\nEvaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: The philosopher Epicurus, and later J.L. Mackie, set out the \"inconsistent triad\": if God is omnipotent and omnibenevolent, God would be able and willing to stop suffering, so the continued existence of suffering suggests such a God does not exist.\n\nA second consideration is: The scale of apparently pointless natural suffering, such as suffering caused by disease or natural disasters that do not result from any human choice, is especially hard to explain if a loving, all-powerful God exists.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: The Irenaean or soul-making theodicy, developed by John Hick, argues that a world containing real challenges and suffering is necessary for humans to develop morally and spiritually into the people God intends them to become; a world with no suffering could not produce courage, compassion or growth.\n\nA further consideration is: The free will defence argues that much suffering results from humans misusing free will rather than from God; and the Book of Job presents a case where suffering is not always a punishment or proof of God's absence, but something whose purpose may be beyond human understanding.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: Christian theodicies try to show suffering is compatible with God's existence (even if mysterious), whereas the problem of evil argues the two cannot be reconciled. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Epicurus / J.L. Mackie's inconsistent triad","Irenaean/soul-making theodicy (John Hick)","Free will defence","Book of Job"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"suffering"},{"id":"c-p1-2-1-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Death and life after death","question":"Choose one religion. Explain what its followers believe about life after death.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Christians believe that Jesus' own resurrection from the dead, described in all four Gospels, is the foundation and guarantee of life after death for believers; 1 Corinthians 15:20 calls Jesus \"the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep\".\n\nSecond, Most Christians believe in a final judgement after death, at which God will judge each person's life, leading to eternal life in heaven with God or separation from God, often called hell.\n\nFinally, Catholic Christians additionally believe in purgatory, a state of purification for those who die in God's friendship but are not yet fully ready for heaven, while most Protestant Christians reject purgatory and emphasise judgement and salvation through faith alone at the point of death.","evidence":["1 Corinthians 15:20–22","Resurrection of Jesus","Final judgement, heaven and hell","Catholic teaching on purgatory"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"afterlife"},{"id":"c-p1-2-1-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Death and life after death","question":"“There is no afterlife.”\nEvaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Humanists and other non-religious people argue that there is no scientific evidence of consciousness surviving death; personality and memory appear to depend entirely on a functioning brain, which suggests death is simply the end of a person's existence.\n\nA second consideration is: If this life is the only one we have, some argue it gives people a strong motive to focus on making this world better and more just, rather than relying on a future life to put things right.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Christians point to the resurrection of Jesus as historical testimony, recorded by multiple witnesses in the Gospels, that death is not the end; this event is treated as the basis for the promise in John 3:16 that \"whoever believes in him shall not perish but have eternal life\".\n\nA further consideration is: Christians also argue that a loving, just God would not allow the apparent injustices of this life, where good people often suffer and wrongdoers sometimes prosper, to be the final word; life after death is needed for ultimate justice.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: this debate often comes down to whether someone trusts the testimony behind religious claims such as the resurrection, or requires independent, empirical evidence before accepting that anything continues beyond physical death. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Resurrection of Jesus","John 3:16","Humanist view of death","1 Corinthians 15"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"afterlife"},{"id":"c-p1-2-2-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The meaning and purpose of life","question":"Choose one religion. Outline two teachings about the meaning and purpose of life.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Many Christians would summarise life's purpose using the Westminster Shorter Catechism's statement that \"man's chief end is to glorify God and to enjoy him forever\", meaning a relationship with God is the central purpose of human life.\n\n2. Jesus taught that the greatest commandments are to love God with all one's heart, soul, mind and strength, and to love one's neighbour as oneself (Mark 12:30–31), so for Christians, life's purpose also involves showing love and service to other people.","evidence":["Westminster Shorter Catechism, Q1","Mark 12:30–31 (Greatest Commandment)","Kingdom of God","Following the example of Jesus"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"meaning-of-life"},{"id":"c-p1-2-2-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The meaning and purpose of life","question":"“A life without God has no purpose.”\nEvaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Many Christians would argue that without God, there is no ultimate, objective source of meaning or value; purpose would simply be invented by individuals and would not outlast death, making it seem arbitrary.\n\nA second consideration is: Christians often root purpose in loving and serving God and in the hope of eternal life, so that even hardship in this life has lasting significance as part of a larger relationship with God.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Humanists strongly reject this, arguing that purpose can come from relationships, creativity, learning, helping others and contributing to human flourishing; the Humanist view is that this one life is precious precisely because it is the only one we have, which gives strong motivation to live it well.\n\nA further consideration is: Many non-religious people live deeply meaningful, ethical lives focused on family, work and community, showing that a sense of purpose does not appear to depend on religious belief in practice.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: whether \"purpose\" requires an eternal or divine foundation to count as genuine, or whether purpose can be built entirely from human relationships and achievements within this one life. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Westminster Shorter Catechism","Humanist Manifesto","Kingdom of God","Secular sources of meaning"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"meaning-of-life"},{"id":"c-p1-2-3-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Sanctity of life, abortion and euthanasia","question":"Choose one religion. Outline two teachings about the sanctity of life in this religion.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Genesis 1:27 teaches that human beings are made \"in the image of God\" (imago Dei), which most Christians believe gives every human life inherent, sacred value that must be respected and protected.\n\n2. The commandment \"You shall not murder\" (Exodus 20:13) is widely used by Christians to argue that human life belongs ultimately to God, so people do not have an unrestricted right to end innocent human life, including at its beginning or end.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27 (imago Dei)","Exodus 20:13","Catholic Catechism on sanctity of life","Life as a gift from God"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"sanctity-of-life"},{"id":"c-p1-2-3-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Sanctity of life, abortion and euthanasia","question":"“Euthanasia should be legalised.”\nEvaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Situation ethics, developed by the Christian thinker Joseph Fletcher, argues that the most loving thing to do should guide moral decisions; some use this to argue that helping a suffering person to die peacefully, at their own request, can be the most compassionate choice.\n\nA second consideration is: Supporters of legalisation also appeal to personal autonomy: individuals should have the right to make decisions about the ending of their own suffering, especially in cases of terminal illness with no hope of recovery.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Most Christian denominations, including the Catholic Church, teach that only God has the right to give and take life (Job 1:21, \"the Lord gave, and the Lord has taken away\"), so euthanasia is viewed as a form of killing that undermines the sanctity of life.\n\nA further consideration is: Opponents also raise the \"slippery slope\" argument, fearing that legal euthanasia could pressure vulnerable, elderly or disabled people to end their lives; many Christians instead point to hospice care, pioneered by the Christian Dame Cicely Saunders, as a compassionate alternative that manages suffering without ending life.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: supporters prioritise compassion and personal choice in the face of suffering, while most Christian teaching prioritises the sanctity of life and trusts God, alongside palliative care, with the timing and manner of death. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Situation ethics (Joseph Fletcher)","Job 1:21","Slippery slope argument","Hospice movement (Cicely Saunders)"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"euthanasia"},{"id":"c-p1-2-4-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Human relationships","question":"Choose one religion. Outline two attitudes its followers hold about heterosexual relationships.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Most Christians teach that marriage between a man and a woman is the proper context for a sexual relationship, based on Genesis 2:24, \"a man shall leave his father and his mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh\".\n\n2. Christians generally see heterosexual relationships within marriage as intended by God for both the expression of love and unity between partners and, traditionally, for having and raising children, though views on strictness vary between denominations.","evidence":["Genesis 2:24 (\"one flesh\")","Marriage as the context for sex","Procreation and unity","Denominational variation on strictness"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"relationships"},{"id":"c-p1-2-4-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.4","moduleTitle":"Human relationships","question":"Choose one religion. Explain why there are different attitudes to homosexuality in this religion.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Some Christians, often described as traditionalist or conservative, cite passages such as Leviticus 18:22 and Romans 1:26–27 as evidence that same-sex sexual relationships are condemned in scripture, and so teach that homosexual relationships are against God's design for marriage.\n\nSecond, Other, more inclusive Christians, including many within the Anglican, United Reformed and Quaker traditions, emphasise Jesus' teaching to love one's neighbour and the equal worth of all people made in God's image, arguing that loving, committed same-sex relationships can be blessed by God.\n\nFinally, Differences also arise because denominations interpret the Bible differently: some read certain Old Testament laws as no longer binding on Christians, while others treat them as timeless moral commands, which is why there is no single Christian position and why views continue to change over time.","evidence":["Leviticus 18:22","Romans 1:26–27","Inclusive church traditions (e.g. Quakers, some Anglicans)","Biblical interpretation and the Great Commandment"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"relationships"},{"id":"c-p1-2-5-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.5","moduleTitle":"Marriage and Partnership","question":"Identify three reasons why marriage may be important to religious people.","marks":3,"command":"Identify","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A clear three-point response could be:\n\n1. It is seen as a sacred covenant made before God, not simply a legal or social arrangement.\n2. It provides what many Christians see as the right context for sexual intimacy and for having and raising children.\n3. For Catholics, marriage is one of the seven sacraments, so it is a channel of God's grace to the couple.","evidence":["Marriage as covenant","Genesis 2:24","Seven sacraments (Catholic)","Wedding vows made before God"],"examTip":"Give three distinct, relevant facts. One clear point earns each mark; development is not required for this question type.","answerTopic":"marriage"},{"id":"c-p1-2-5-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.5","moduleTitle":"Marriage and Partnership","question":"Choose one religion. Explain why marriage is important for members of that religion.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, For most Christians, marriage is a covenant relationship rather than just a contract, understood as mirroring the faithful, self-giving relationship between Christ and the Church, as described in Ephesians 5:25, \"husbands, love your wives, as Christ loved the church\".\n\nSecond, Marriage is traditionally seen as the proper setting within which sexual intimacy and the bringing up of children should take place, giving structure and stability to family life and reflecting God's intention from creation (Genesis 2:24).\n\nFinally, For Catholic Christians, marriage is one of the seven sacraments, meaning it is believed to convey God's grace to the couple, which is why the marriage ceremony and the vows exchanged are treated as sacred and, ideally, lifelong.","evidence":["Ephesians 5:25","Genesis 2:24","Marriage as a sacrament","Lifelong commitment / vows"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"marriage"},{"id":"c-p1-2-6-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.6","moduleTitle":"Divorce and Remarriage","question":"Choose one religion. Outline two attitudes to divorce.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Jesus taught in Mark 10:2–9 that marriage is intended by God to be a permanent union, concluding \"what God has joined together, let no one separate\"; many Christians, especially the Catholic Church, use this teaching to oppose divorce and instead offer annulment in certain limited circumstances.\n\n2. Many Protestant churches permit divorce, particularly where there has been adultery, abuse or the irretrievable breakdown of a marriage, emphasising God's forgiveness and the pastoral need to allow people a fresh start rather than treating every divorce as sinful.","evidence":["Mark 10:2–9","Matthew 19:3–9 (exception clause)","Catholic teaching on annulment","Protestant pastoral approaches"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"divorce"},{"id":"c-p1-2-6-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.6","moduleTitle":"Divorce and Remarriage","question":"Choose one religion. Explain the attitudes its followers have towards remarriage.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, The Catholic Church generally does not recognise remarriage in church while a former spouse is still alive, because it holds that a valid sacramental marriage cannot be dissolved; a Catholic seeking to remarry in church would normally need to obtain an annulment, which declares that no valid marriage existed in the first place.\n\nSecond, The Church of England changed its policy in 2002 so that, at the discretion of the local vicar, divorced people may be remarried in church, reflecting a pastoral balance between upholding the ideal of lifelong marriage and recognising human failure and the possibility of a fresh start.\n\nFinally, Many other Protestant churches are generally more willing to bless remarriage, especially where the first marriage ended through circumstances such as adultery or abuse, emphasising God's grace and the belief that people should not be permanently excluded from marriage because of past breakdown.","evidence":["Catholic annulment","Church of England 2002 policy change","Grace and forgiveness","Exception clause, Matthew 19:9"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"remarriage"},{"id":"c-p1-2-7-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.7","moduleTitle":"Family Structures and Responsibilities","question":"Explain why family life is important for many religious people.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Deuteronomy 6:6–7 instructs parents to teach God's commandments diligently to their children, \"when you sit in your house, and when you walk by the way\", showing that the family is traditionally seen as the first and most important place where faith is passed on.\n\nSecond, Ephesians 5–6 sets out mutual duties within the household, including love between husband and wife and the honouring of parents by children, so family relationships are understood as part of living out Christian faith in daily life, not separate from it.\n\nFinally, Through practices such as infant baptism, saying grace, family prayer and attending church together, family life gives children a direct experience of Christian community and values, helping the next generation to grow in faith.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 6:6–7","Ephesians 5–6 (household codes)","Infant baptism and godparents","Family worship and prayer"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"family"},{"id":"c-p1-2-7-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.7","moduleTitle":"Family Structures and Responsibilities","question":"“Children should be brought up to be religious.” Evaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Many Christians argue that Ephesians 6:4 instructs parents to \"bring [children] up in the discipline and instruction of the Lord\", which they see as a parental duty rather than an optional choice, since faith shapes moral values and gives life meaning.\n\nA second consideration is: Through infant baptism, godparents and the wider church community formally commit to supporting a child's religious upbringing, showing that it is viewed as a shared responsibility that benefits both the child and the community.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Humanists and some liberal religious thinkers argue that children should be allowed to explore different beliefs and decide for themselves once they are old enough to understand the choice, rather than being taught one religion as if it were simply true.\n\nA further consideration is: Critics of compulsory religious upbringing argue it can amount to indoctrination if children are not also exposed to other viewpoints, and that good moral character and a sense of purpose can be developed without religious belief, for example through Humanist approaches to ethics.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: this often depends on whether religious upbringing is seen primarily as passing on loving guidance and identity, or as limiting a child's freedom to form their own beliefs. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Ephesians 6:4","Infant baptism and godparents","Humanist approach to ethics","Concerns about indoctrination"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"family"},{"id":"c-p1-2-8-0","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.8","moduleTitle":"Childlessness and Celibacy","question":"Choose one religion. Outline two beliefs about celibacy.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. St Paul teaches in 1 Corinthians 7:32–35 that an unmarried person can be \"anxious about the things of the Lord\" without the competing responsibilities of marriage, so celibacy is valued by many Christians as a way of devoting oneself fully to God's work.\n\n2. In the Catholic tradition, priests, monks and nuns are generally required to remain celibate, following what is seen as the example of Jesus himself, who did not marry, and symbolising total dedication to the Church; most Protestant traditions do not require clergy to be celibate.","evidence":["1 Corinthians 7:32–35","Catholic clerical celibacy","Jesus as an unmarried example","Monastic vocation"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"celibacy"},{"id":"c-p1-2-8-1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.8","moduleTitle":"Childlessness and Celibacy","question":"“There is no point to celibacy.” Evaluate this statement.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Some argue that celibacy goes against a basic human need for intimacy and family life, and that most Protestant traditions, which do not require clerical celibacy, show that close devotion to God does not actually require giving up marriage.\n\nA second consideration is: Humanists and other critics may see enforced celibacy, such as in some cases of clerical abuse scandals, as having caused serious harm, arguing this shows celibacy can create more problems than it solves.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Catholic and monastic tradition sees celibacy as a genuine vocation and gift, following 1 Corinthians 7, which frees a person from family responsibilities so they can devote themselves fully and without distraction to prayer, ministry and service to others.\n\nA further consideration is: Celibate figures such as monks, nuns and many priests have historically contributed enormously to education, care for the poor and the sick, and the spiritual life of the Church, which many see as clear evidence that celibacy has served a valuable and far from pointless purpose.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: whether celibacy is judged by whether it suits every individual, or by whether it has value as one legitimate vocation among several ways of serving God. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["1 Corinthians 7:32–35","Catholic clerical celibacy","Monastic vocation","Criticism of enforced celibacy"],"examTip":"For a 12-mark evaluation, use accurate religious teaching, explain different religious or non-religious views, analyse the statement and reach a justified conclusion. The conclusion should follow from the arguments you have discussed.","answerTopic":"celibacy"}],"paper2":[{"id":"c-p2-1-1-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Jesus: the incarnate Son of God","question":"Outline two beliefs about Jesus as the incarnate Son of God.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. The incarnation is the belief that God the Son became fully human in Jesus Christ while remaining fully divine, summarised in John 1:14, \"the Word became flesh and dwelt among us\".\n\n2. Christians believe the virgin birth, in which Jesus was conceived by the Holy Spirit and born to Mary (Luke 1:26–35), shows that Jesus was uniquely both divine and human from the very beginning of his life.","evidence":["John 1:14 (\"the Word became flesh\")","Luke 1:26–35 (virgin birth)","Nicene Creed","Fully God and fully human"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"incarnation"},{"id":"c-p2-1-1-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Jesus: the incarnate Son of God","question":"Explain why the incarnation is important for Christians.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, The incarnation shows that God chose to enter human history and experience human life directly in Jesus, which Christians believe demonstrates the depth of God's love for humanity, as summarised in John 3:16, \"God so loved the world that he gave his only Son\".\n\nSecond, Because Jesus is believed to be fully human, Christians see him as able to fully understand human suffering and temptation (Hebrews 4:15), while because he is fully God, his teaching, death and resurrection carry divine authority and saving power.\n\nFinally, The incarnation underpins the Christian doctrine of salvation: Jesus' death on the cross is understood as an atoning sacrifice that could only fully reconcile humanity with God because Jesus was both truly human, able to represent humanity, and truly divine, able to overcome sin and death.","evidence":["John 3:16","Hebrews 4:15","Atonement","Nicene Creed"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"incarnation"},{"id":"c-p2-1-1-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"Jesus: the incarnate Son of God","question":"Discuss whether the resurrection is the most important belief about Jesus for Christians.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: St Paul states in 1 Corinthians 15:14 that \"if Christ has not been raised, then our preaching is in vain and your faith is in vain\", suggesting the resurrection is the foundation on which all other Christian belief depends.\n\nA second consideration is: The resurrection is celebrated as the most important festival in the Christian calendar, Easter, and is treated as the guarantee of eternal life for believers, giving hope in the face of death.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Some would argue the incarnation is equally fundamental, since without God becoming human in Jesus there would be no resurrection to speak of; others point to Jesus' teaching and example of self-giving love, especially his death on the cross, as what most directly shapes Christian moral life day to day.\n\nA further consideration is: The crucifixion itself is sometimes highlighted as central because it demonstrates the extent of God's love and the atonement for sin, which some argue is the belief most directly relevant to how Christians understand forgiveness.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: incarnation, crucifixion and resurrection are closely interconnected beliefs, and different Christians and denominations place slightly different weight on each depending on whether they emphasise God's love, atonement, or hope for eternal life. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["1 Corinthians 15:14","Easter","Incarnation and crucifixion","Atonement"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"incarnation"},{"id":"c-p2-1-2-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The Bible and its authority","question":"Outline two reasons why the Bible is important for Christians.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. The Bible is believed to be inspired by God (2 Timothy 3:16, \"all Scripture is breathed out by God\"), so Christians treat it as a primary source of teaching about God's character and will for human life.\n\n2. The Bible contains the Gospels, which record the life, teachings, death and resurrection of Jesus, giving Christians the central story and example that shapes their faith and moral conduct.","evidence":["2 Timothy 3:16","The Gospels","Old and New Testament","Scripture used in worship"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"bible"},{"id":"c-p2-1-2-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The Bible and its authority","question":"Explain why Christians have different views about the authority of the Bible.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Some Christians, often described as biblical literalists or fundamentalists, believe the Bible is the inerrant, literal word of God and should be followed exactly as written, including on matters such as the six-day creation.\n\nSecond, Other Christians take a more liberal approach, believing the Bible was written by human authors inspired by God within a particular historical and cultural context, so some passages should be understood symbolically or reinterpreted for the modern world rather than applied literally.\n\nFinally, Denominational differences also affect authority: Catholic Christians hold that Church tradition and the teaching authority of the Magisterium work alongside scripture, while many Protestant Christians hold to \"sola scriptura\", the belief that the Bible alone is the ultimate authority for faith and practice.","evidence":["Biblical literalism","Liberal/symbolic interpretation","Sola scriptura (Protestant)","Church tradition and Magisterium (Catholic)"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"bible"},{"id":"c-p2-1-2-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The Bible and its authority","question":"Discuss whether the Bible should be read literally.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Literalists argue that because the Bible is the inspired word of God, it should be trusted and followed exactly as written; treating parts of it as merely symbolic risks undermining its authority and allowing people to pick and choose which teachings to accept.\n\nA second consideration is: A literal approach to key texts, such as the resurrection accounts, is seen by some as essential, since if central historical claims are not literally true, the foundations of Christian faith would be seriously weakened.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Other Christians argue that parts of the Bible, such as parables told by Jesus or poetic sections like the Psalms, were never intended to be read as literal history, and that forcing a literal reading onto all of scripture misunderstands its different types of writing (genres).\n\nA further consideration is: Taking some passages literally can also create tension with scientific understanding, such as evolution and the age of the universe, leading many mainstream Christians to adopt theistic evolution, reading Genesis as teaching that God created the world without requiring a literal six days.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: the answer often depends on which parts of the Bible are being discussed (history, law, poetry, parable), and whether a Christian's denomination emphasises scriptural inerrancy or interpretation guided by tradition and reason. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Biblical literalism","Genres of scripture (parable, poetry, history)","Theistic evolution","Resurrection accounts"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"bible"},{"id":"c-p2-1-3-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Leadership in the Church","question":"Outline two features of ordained ministry in the Christian Church.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Ordained ministers, such as priests or ministers, are formally set apart through ordination, often involving the laying on of hands, to lead worship, preach, teach and administer sacraments such as the Eucharist.\n\n2. In the Catholic and Orthodox traditions, ordained ministry follows a hierarchy including deacons, priests and bishops, with the Pope as leader of the worldwide Catholic Church, while many Protestant churches have simpler structures with ministers elected or appointed by the local congregation.","evidence":["Ordination / laying on of hands","Apostolic succession","Catholic hierarchy (deacon, priest, bishop, Pope)","Protestant congregational leadership"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"leadership"},{"id":"c-p2-1-3-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Leadership in the Church","question":"Explain why there are different forms of leadership in the Christian Church.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Catholic and Orthodox churches maintain apostolic succession, the belief that bishops are successors of Jesus' original apostles, giving their leadership structure, with the Pope at its head, a particular claim to continuity and authority going back to the early Church.\n\nSecond, Many Protestant churches rejected parts of this hierarchy during the Reformation, emphasising instead the \"priesthood of all believers\", the idea that every Christian has direct access to God without necessarily needing an ordained priest to mediate, leading to simpler, often more congregation-led leadership.\n\nFinally, Differences also arise over the ordination of women: some denominations, including many Anglican and Protestant churches, now ordain women as priests or ministers, while the Catholic and Orthodox churches continue to ordain only men, reflecting different interpretations of scripture and tradition.","evidence":["Apostolic succession","Priesthood of all believers","The Reformation","Ordination of women"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"leadership"},{"id":"c-p2-1-3-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Leadership in the Church","question":"Discuss whether only ordained leaders should lead Christian worship.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Many Christians argue that ordination sets a person apart through training, vocation and the laying on of hands, giving them particular authority and responsibility to administer sacraments such as the Eucharist and to guide a congregation reliably in doctrine.\n\nA second consideration is: Having recognised, ordained leadership can help maintain consistency and unity across a denomination, ensuring that worship and teaching remain faithful to that church's beliefs rather than varying unpredictably from person to person.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Other Christians, especially in traditions that stress the \"priesthood of all believers\", argue that since the Holy Spirit can work through any believer, lay people should be able to lead prayer, preach and even lead small worship gatherings, not only ordained clergy.\n\nA further consideration is: In practice, many churches already rely heavily on lay leadership for activities such as leading prayer groups, Sunday school and pastoral visiting, showing that meaningful Christian worship and ministry do not always require ordination.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: the answer often depends on whether the focus is on sacramental acts traditionally reserved for ordained leaders, or on the wider life of prayer, teaching and service where lay involvement is already widespread and valued. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Priesthood of all believers","Ordination and sacraments","Lay leadership","Apostolic succession"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"leadership"},{"id":"c-p2-1-4-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"One other significant Christian","question":"Outline two actions of Martin Luther King Jr which show his significance to Christians.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. As a Baptist minister, Martin Luther King Jr led the Civil Rights Movement in the United States using non-violent protest, including the Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955–56), grounding his campaign explicitly in Christian teaching about love, justice and human dignity.\n\n2. In his \"I Have a Dream\" speech (1963) and his leadership of the March on Washington, King drew on biblical imagery, such as the prophets' call for justice, to argue that racial equality was a moral and religious obligation, not just a political goal.","evidence":["Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955–56)","\"I Have a Dream\" speech (1963)","Non-violent protest","Nobel Peace Prize (1964)"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"significant-christian"},{"id":"c-p2-1-4-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"One other significant Christian","question":"Explain why Martin Luther King Jr is a significant figure for Christians.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, King based his commitment to non-violent resistance on Jesus' teaching to \"turn the other cheek\" (Matthew 5:39) and on the example of love for enemies, showing Christians a model of how faith can directly inspire social and political action.\n\nSecond, His work in the Civil Rights Movement is seen by many Christians as an example of working for the Kingdom of God on earth, applying biblical teaching about justice, equality and the worth of every person made in the image of God to a real, urgent social problem.\n\nFinally, King's willingness to suffer, be imprisoned and eventually be assassinated (1968) for his beliefs is often compared by Christians to the self-sacrifice of Jesus, making him an enduring example of costly Christian discipleship and moral courage.","evidence":["Matthew 5:39 (\"turn the other cheek\")","Imago Dei and equality","Letter from Birmingham Jail (1963)","Assassination in 1968"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"significant-christian"},{"id":"c-p2-1-4-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"One other significant Christian","question":"Discuss whether Martin Luther King Jr is the most significant Christian of the twentieth century.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: King's leadership of the Civil Rights Movement had a lasting impact on law and society in the United States, and his explicitly Christian, non-violent approach to justice continues to inspire campaigners and churches worldwide.\n\nA second consideration is: His writings and speeches, grounded in scripture, show how Christian faith can be applied to major social issues, making him a powerful example of practical discipleship rather than only private belief.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Other twentieth-century Christians could be argued as equally or more significant in different ways, for example Mother Teresa's decades of direct care for the poorest in Calcutta, or Pope John Paul II's influence on global Catholicism and his role in the fall of communism in Eastern Europe.\n\nA further consideration is: Significance can be measured differently: King is especially significant for linking Christian faith to political and social justice, while others are significant chiefly through acts of personal charity or through leadership of the wider Church.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: judging who is \"most\" significant depends on whether significance is measured by social and political impact, by direct charitable service, or by influence on the institutional Church, and different Christians would reasonably disagree. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Civil Rights Movement","Mother Teresa","Pope John Paul II","Christian social action"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"significant-christian"},{"id":"c-p2-2-1-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Christmas and Easter","question":"Outline two ways in which Christians celebrate Easter.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Many Christians attend a special Easter Sunday service celebrating Jesus' resurrection, often including joyful hymns, the lighting of the Paschal (Easter) candle and the greeting \"Christ is risen! He is risen indeed!\"\n\n2. The period of Lent, forty days of fasting, prayer and reflection leading up to Easter, and Holy Week, including Maundy Thursday (the Last Supper), Good Friday (the crucifixion) and Holy Saturday, prepare Christians spiritually to fully celebrate Easter Sunday.","evidence":["Lent (40 days)","Holy Week: Maundy Thursday, Good Friday, Easter Sunday","Paschal candle","\"Christ is risen!\" greeting"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"easter"},{"id":"c-p2-2-1-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Christmas and Easter","question":"Explain why Easter is the most important festival for Christians.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Easter celebrates the resurrection of Jesus from the dead, which St Paul describes as the very foundation of Christian faith (1 Corinthians 15:14), since without the resurrection Christians believe their faith would be meaningless.\n\nSecond, The resurrection is understood as proof that Jesus overcame sin and death, giving Christians hope and assurance of their own resurrection and eternal life, which is a central promise of the Christian faith.\n\nFinally, Unlike Christmas, which celebrates Jesus' birth, Easter marks the completion of Jesus' saving work; this is why Holy Week and Easter are given such prominence in the Church calendar, with many churches treating Easter, not Christmas, as the most theologically significant festival.","evidence":["1 Corinthians 15:14","Resurrection as foundation of faith","Holy Week","Easter Vigil"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"easter"},{"id":"c-p2-2-1-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Christmas and Easter","question":"Discuss whether Christmas has lost its religious meaning.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: In many countries, Christmas has become heavily commercialised, with an emphasis on gift-giving, decorations and parties that have little connection to the Christian story of Jesus' birth, which some Christians feel has overshadowed the festival's original meaning.\n\nA second consideration is: Surveys suggest that a large proportion of people who celebrate Christmas do not attend a religious service or reflect on the incarnation, focusing instead on family traditions that are largely secular in practice.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Many Christians would respond that churches remain full for events such as Midnight Mass and carol services, and that for believers, Christmas retains its core meaning as a celebration of the incarnation, God becoming human in Jesus, regardless of the surrounding secular culture.\n\nA further consideration is: Some Christians also see opportunities within the wider cultural celebration of Christmas, such as carol singing, nativity plays and charitable giving, as ways the Christian message can still reach people who might not otherwise engage with church.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: whether Christmas has \"lost\" its meaning may depend on whether the focus is on wider secular culture, where commercial elements dominate, or on practising Christians and churches, for whom the incarnation remains central. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Commercialisation of Christmas","Midnight Mass","Incarnation","Carol services and nativity plays"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"christmas"},{"id":"c-p2-2-2-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage in Christianity","question":"Outline two features of pilgrimage to Lourdes.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. Lourdes in France became a site of Catholic pilgrimage after Bernadette Soubirous reported visions of the Virgin Mary in 1858; many pilgrims visit the grotto where the apparitions are said to have occurred.\n\n2. Pilgrims bathe in or drink water from the spring at Lourdes, believed by many to have healing properties, and the sick and disabled are specially welcomed, often assisted by volunteers, reflecting the site's strong association with healing and hope.","evidence":["Bernadette Soubirous (1858)","The Grotto at Lourdes","Healing spring water","Care of the sick and disabled pilgrims"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"pilgrimage"},{"id":"c-p2-2-2-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage in Christianity","question":"Explain why Jerusalem is an important place of pilgrimage for Christians.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Jerusalem contains sites directly connected to the final days of Jesus' life, including the Via Dolorosa, the traditional route Jesus walked to his crucifixion, allowing pilgrims to retrace his final journey.\n\nSecond, The Church of the Holy Sepulchre is built on the site traditionally believed to be both the location of Jesus' crucifixion (Golgotha) and his tomb, making it one of the holiest sites in Christianity and a major focus of pilgrimage, especially during Holy Week.\n\nFinally, Visiting Jerusalem allows Christians to feel a direct, physical connection to the events described in the Gospels, deepening their faith and giving them a renewed sense of the reality of Jesus' life, death and resurrection.","evidence":["Via Dolorosa","Church of the Holy Sepulchre","Golgotha","Holy Week pilgrimage"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"pilgrimage"},{"id":"c-p2-2-2-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage in Christianity","question":"Discuss whether pilgrimage is essential for Christians.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Visiting sites such as Jerusalem or Lourdes can deepen a Christian's faith by connecting them physically to the events of Jesus' life or to places associated with healing and devotion, often described by pilgrims as a uniquely powerful spiritual experience.\n\nA second consideration is: Pilgrimage can also strengthen a sense of community and shared tradition, as pilgrims often travel and worship together, and some seek specific blessings, healing or an opportunity for focused prayer and reflection away from ordinary life.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: However, pilgrimage is not a requirement of core Christian doctrine or the sacraments, and is often expensive and physically demanding, meaning many devout Christians never go on pilgrimage yet maintain a strong faith through prayer, worship and scripture at home.\n\nA further consideration is: Some Christians argue that God can be encountered anywhere, so focusing on travel to particular holy places risks suggesting, wrongly, that God is more present in some locations than others.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: pilgrimage can be a valuable and meaningful spiritual practice for those who undertake it, but it is best understood as an optional enrichment of faith rather than an essential requirement, since core Christian practices such as prayer and worship do not depend on it. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Lourdes and Jerusalem","Personal prayer at home","Community pilgrimage","God's presence everywhere"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"pilgrimage"},{"id":"c-p2-3-1-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.1","moduleTitle":"Churches: design and features","question":"Outline two features of a Catholic church building.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. The altar is the focal point of a Catholic church, where the priest celebrates the Eucharist, and is often positioned so the congregation can clearly see the celebration of the sacrament.\n\n2. Many Catholic churches contain statues or images of Mary and the saints, along with the Stations of the Cross around the walls, depicting the events of Jesus' crucifixion, which believers use to aid prayer and reflection.","evidence":["The altar","Stations of the Cross","Statues of Mary and saints","Tabernacle (reserved Eucharist)"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"churches"},{"id":"c-p2-3-1-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.1","moduleTitle":"Churches: design and features","question":"Explain why churches are designed and arranged in different ways.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Catholic and Orthodox churches are often richly decorated with statues, icons and stained glass, reflecting a theology that sees beauty and visual imagery as aids to worship and as a way of teaching biblical stories to worshippers.\n\nSecond, Many Protestant churches, especially those shaped by the Reformation, deliberately keep their buildings plainer, with the pulpit (for preaching) often more prominent than the altar/table, reflecting the Protestant emphasis on the centrality of scripture and preaching over visual imagery.\n\nFinally, The layout of a church can also reflect its style of worship: a Quaker meeting house, for example, has simple seating arranged so worshippers face one another, supporting their tradition of silent worship, rather than the long, altar-facing naves of more liturgical traditions.","evidence":["Catholic/Orthodox iconography","Protestant pulpit-centred design","Quaker meeting houses","Reformation influence on simplicity"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"churches"},{"id":"c-p2-3-1-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.1","moduleTitle":"Churches: design and features","question":"Discuss whether church buildings are necessary for Christian worship.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Church buildings provide a dedicated, set-apart space for communal worship, housing items needed for sacraments such as the altar for the Eucharist and the font for baptism, and offering continuity and stability for a worshipping community.\n\nA second consideration is: Historic and beautifully designed churches can themselves inspire worship and a sense of the sacred, and act as visible symbols of Christian presence and heritage within a community.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Jesus taught that God can be worshipped \"in spirit and in truth\" rather than only in one particular place (John 4:21–24), and many Christians worship successfully in homes, online, in prisons or in the open air, especially where church buildings are unavailable or persecution makes them dangerous to use.\n\nA further consideration is: The early Church met largely in homes before church buildings became common, showing historically that a building is not essential for Christian community, worship or the celebration of sacraments to take place.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: church buildings are valuable and practical for organising regular communal worship and sacraments, but they are not doctrinally essential, since Christians have always been able to worship God in a wide variety of settings. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["John 4:21–24","Early house churches","Persecuted church worship","Altar and font"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"churches"},{"id":"c-p2-3-2-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.2","moduleTitle":"Christian worship, prayer and the Eucharist","question":"Outline two features of the Eucharist.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. The Eucharist re-enacts the Last Supper, where Jesus shared bread and wine with his disciples and said \"this is my body\" and \"this is my blood\" (Mark 14:22–24), using bread and wine as central symbols of Christ's sacrifice.\n\n2. Catholics believe in transubstantiation, that the bread and wine actually become the body and blood of Christ, while many Protestants see the bread and wine as a symbolic or spiritual remembrance of Jesus' sacrifice rather than a literal change.","evidence":["Mark 14:22–24 (the Last Supper)","Transubstantiation (Catholic)","Symbolic memorial view (Protestant)","Bread and wine"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"eucharist"},{"id":"c-p2-3-2-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.2","moduleTitle":"Christian worship, prayer and the Eucharist","question":"Explain why prayer is important for Christians.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Prayer is understood by Christians as direct communication with God, following Jesus' own example and his teaching of the Lord's Prayer (Matthew 6:9–13), which gives a model for how Christians should approach God.\n\nSecond, Through prayer, Christians express praise, confession of sin, thanksgiving and requests (sometimes summarised by the acronym ACTS), helping to build and maintain a personal relationship with God in daily life.\n\nFinally, Prayer can be offered individually or corporately in church worship, and many Christians see it as a source of comfort, guidance and strength, particularly in times of difficulty, since it reflects trust that God hears and responds to those who pray.","evidence":["Matthew 6:9–13 (the Lord's Prayer)","ACTS model of prayer","Personal and corporate prayer","Prayer as relationship with God"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"prayer"},{"id":"c-p2-3-2-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.2","moduleTitle":"Christian worship, prayer and the Eucharist","question":"Discuss whether private worship is more important than public worship for Christians.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Private worship, such as personal prayer and Bible reading, allows an individual to build and deepen a personal relationship with God, following Jesus' own example of withdrawing to pray alone (Luke 5:16), and can happen at any time without needing other people present.\n\nA second consideration is: Private prayer can be particularly important for Christians who are unable to attend church, for example due to illness, persecution or living in an area with no church community.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Public worship brings Christians together as the \"body of Christ\" (1 Corinthians 12), allowing them to share sacraments such as the Eucharist, which many denominations see as requiring a gathered community and an ordained minister, and to support and encourage one another in faith.\n\nA further consideration is: Hebrews 10:25 explicitly instructs believers not to neglect meeting together, suggesting that communal worship has a scriptural importance that purely private devotion cannot fully replace.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: private and public worship generally complement rather than compete with one another, but where a choice must be made, most Christian traditions see regular public worship, including the sacraments, as carrying a distinct and scripturally grounded importance. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Luke 5:16","Hebrews 10:25","1 Corinthians 12 (body of Christ)","Personal and corporate prayer"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"worship"},{"id":"c-p2-3-3-0","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.3","moduleTitle":"Rites of passage in Christian life","question":"Outline two features of a Christian baptism.","marks":4,"command":"Outline","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A strong response develops two separate points:\n\n1. In infant baptism, common in Catholic, Orthodox and many Protestant churches, a baby is baptised with water, often poured over the head, while godparents and parents make promises on the child's behalf to raise them in the Christian faith.\n\n2. In believer's baptism, practised by Baptists and some other denominations, a person who is old enough to personally profess faith is fully immersed in water, symbolising dying to their old life and rising to new life in Christ (Romans 6:4).","evidence":["Infant baptism and godparents","Believer's baptism (full immersion)","Romans 6:4 (dying and rising with Christ)","Water as a symbol of cleansing"],"examTip":"Make two separate points and develop each one. Link the development directly to the question.","answerTopic":"baptism"},{"id":"c-p2-3-3-1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.3","moduleTitle":"Rites of passage in Christian life","question":"Explain why confirmation is important for many Christians.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A developed answer could explain the issue through three connected points.\n\nFirst, Confirmation allows a person who was baptised as an infant to personally confirm the promises that were made on their behalf by parents and godparents, taking on the Christian faith as their own choice rather than one made for them.\n\nSecond, In the Catholic and Orthodox traditions, confirmation is understood as a sacrament in which the Holy Spirit strengthens the believer, often with the bishop laying hands on and anointing the candidate with oil (chrism), echoing the gifts of the Holy Spirit at Pentecost (Acts 2).\n\nFinally, Confirmation is often seen as a significant milestone marking the transition to full, mature membership of the church community, after which a person can fully participate in receiving the Eucharist in many traditions.","evidence":["Confirmation of baptismal promises","Anointing with chrism oil","Acts 2 (Pentecost)","Full participation in the Eucharist"],"examTip":"For 6 marks, combine accurate knowledge with explanation. Explain why each point matters and link it directly to the question; avoid simply listing facts.","answerTopic":"confirmation"},{"id":"c-p2-3-3-2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.3","moduleTitle":"Rites of passage in Christian life","question":"Discuss whether baptism should only be performed on believing adults.","marks":10,"command":"Discuss","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A FIRST PERSPECTIVE\n\nOne line of reasoning is: Baptists and some other denominations argue that baptism should follow a personal, conscious profession of faith, since the New Testament examples of baptism, such as the Ethiopian official in Acts 8:36–38, show adults choosing baptism for themselves after believing.\n\nA second consideration is: Believer's baptism, they argue, better reflects the meaning of baptism as a response to and public declaration of one's own faith, rather than a decision made by parents on a child's behalf before the child can understand it.\n\nAN ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVE\n\nA different perspective is: Most Catholic, Orthodox and many other Protestant churches baptise infants, arguing this reflects God's grace reaching out to a person before they can respond, similar to how children were included in the Old Testament covenant through circumcision, and relies on parents and godparents to nurture the child's faith until confirmation.\n\nA further consideration is: Infant baptism also welcomes a child fully into the church community and family of faith from the very beginning of life, which these traditions see as a gift rather than something that should wait until later understanding is possible.\n\nJUDGEMENT\n\nOverall, the conclusion should weigh the arguments above against this key consideration: this is a genuine and long-standing denominational disagreement, with Baptist traditions emphasising the necessity of personal, believing faith at baptism, and other major traditions emphasising God's grace and the inclusion of children within the believing community from birth. It should then state which perspective is more convincing in relation to the exact wording of the question and explain why, rather than assuming that every Christian will reach the same view.","evidence":["Acts 8:36–38","Believer's baptism (Baptists)","Infant baptism","Confirmation"],"examTip":"For a 10-mark discussion, analyse different aspects of the Christian issue and how they interrelate. Finish with a reasoned conclusion; do not just list advantages and disadvantages.","answerTopic":"baptism"}]},"notes":[{"id":"c-p1-1.1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.1","title":"The universe and the place of human beings","specFocus":"Christian teaching on the origins of the universe and the place and responsibility of human beings within creation.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.1 The Universe and the Place of Human Beings\nChristians believe the universe was created by God, and that human beings hold a special, responsible place within it.\nTwo creation accounts in Genesis\nGenesis 1: God speaks the universe into being over six days — light, sky, land and plants, sun and moon, sea and sky creatures, land animals, and finally human beings \"in our image, after our likeness\" — before resting on the seventh day.\nGenesis 2: God forms Adam from the dust of the ground and breathes life into him; Eve is later formed from Adam's rib. This account stresses God's personal, hands-on involvement in making humanity.\n• Many Christians read these chapters as theological truth about who created the world and why, rather than as a scientific textbook.\n• Young Earth creationists read Genesis literally, holding that God created the world in six literal days relatively recently.\n• Theistic evolutionists (for example the BioLogos movement) accept the scientific evidence for the Big Bang and evolution, while believing God is the ultimate cause and purpose behind these natural processes.\nImago Dei and stewardship\n• Imago Dei (\"image of God\") is the belief that every human being reflects something of God's nature — reason, creativity, moral responsibility and the capacity to love — giving human life unique value and dignity.\n• Genesis 1:28 gives humans \"dominion\" over the earth and its creatures; most Christians interpret this as a call to responsible stewardship rather than a licence to exploit nature, since the earth remains God's creation, not human property.\n• Many Christian environmental movements (e.g. A Rocha, Christian Aid's climate work) appeal to stewardship to argue that caring for creation is a religious duty, not just a practical necessity.\nScience and religion\n• The cosmological argument (Thomas Aquinas) argues that everything which exists has a cause; since an endless chain of causes is thought impossible, there must be an uncaused first cause, which believers identify as God.\n• The Big Bang theory and evolution by natural selection offer scientific explanations of origins; many Christians see these as compatible with, rather than opposed to, belief in a creator God working through natural law.\nExam link: when asked to \"choose one religion\", use Genesis, imago Dei and stewardship; contrast with non-religious accounts such as the Big Bang and evolution when a question asks for both religious and non-religious views."},{"id":"c-p1-1.2","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.2","title":"Human nature and the human condition","specFocus":"Christian beliefs about what it means to be human, the nature of the soul, and whether human beings survive bodily death.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.2 Human Nature and the Human Condition\nChristians generally believe human beings are more than physical bodies: each person has a soul, made in the image of God, which gives them lasting spiritual significance.\nBody and soul\n• Genesis 2:7 describes God breathing \"the breath of life\" into Adam, who then \"became a living being\" — many Christians take this to show that human life and identity are God-given, not purely biological.\n• Imago Dei: being made in God's image is understood to include the capacity for reason, moral choice, creativity and relationship — qualities thought to distinguish humans from animals.\n• Most Christians believe the soul survives physical death and will ultimately be reunited with a transformed, resurrected body (1 Corinthians 15), rather than existing forever as a separate spirit alone.\nHuman weakness\n• Christians also recognise a darker side to human nature: Original Sin, traced to Adam and Eve's disobedience in Genesis 3, is held to have left all humans with an inclination towards selfishness and wrongdoing.\n• This creates tension in Christian anthropology: humans are simultaneously described as made in God's image (dignified, valuable) and as fallen (flawed, sinful) — both ideas shape Christian ethics and the need for salvation through Christ.\nNon-religious views\n• Humanists reject the idea of an immortal soul, arguing personality, memory and consciousness arise entirely from brain activity, and that human dignity and morality do not require belief in God or an afterlife.\nExam link: questions often ask you to contrast \"religious\" and \"non-religious\" beliefs about human nature — keep the Christian soul/imago Dei view clearly separate from the humanist, brain-based view."},{"id":"c-p1-1.3","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.3","title":"Selfishness, greed, ignorance and sin","specFocus":"Christian teaching on sin, the Fall, and how Christians try to overcome selfishness, greed, ignorance and wrongdoing.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.3 Selfishness, Greed, Ignorance and Sin\nSin is central to Christian teaching about the human condition: it describes anything that falls short of God's standards and damages a person's relationship with God and with others.\nThe Fall and Original Sin\n• Genesis 3 tells of Adam and Eve disobeying God's one command by eating fruit from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, despite the serpent's temptation. This act, the Fall, is traditionally understood to have introduced sin and death into the world.\n• Original Sin is the belief that every human inherits this tendency towards selfishness and wrongdoing; Romans 3:23 summarises this starkly: \"all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God\".\nForms of wrongdoing\n• Selfishness and greed: putting one's own wants above the needs of others or of God, condemned throughout the Bible, for example in the Ten Commandments' prohibition on coveting (Exodus 20:17).\n• Ignorance: failing to know or follow God's will, sometimes through a hardened conscience rather than a simple lack of information.\n• Sin as \"missing the mark\": the biblical concept of sin (Greek hamartia) pictures wrongdoing as falling short of a target — God's standard of holiness and love — rather than only breaking specific rules.\nOvercoming sin\n• Christians believe forgiveness and reconciliation with God are possible through confession, repentance (turning away from sin) and faith in Jesus' death and resurrection, which Christians believe atones for sin.\n• Regular practices such as confession (especially in the Catholic Church), prayer and following Jesus' teaching and example are seen as ways of resisting selfishness and growing in goodness.\nExam link: for \"Identify\" questions, give specific concrete examples (e.g. theft, pride, dishonesty); for \"Explain\" questions, connect sin back to the Fall and to the need for forgiveness through Christ."},{"id":"c-p1-1.4","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.4","title":"Free will, determinism and predestination","specFocus":"Christian debate about whether humans are genuinely free, and the doctrine of predestination found in some traditions.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.4 Free Will, Determinism and Predestination\nThis module explores whether human beings are truly free to choose, or whether their choices are determined, perhaps even by God.\nFree will\n• Most Christians believe God gave humans free will so that love, faith and obedience would be freely chosen, not forced — a forced \"love\" would not be real love at all.\n• Genesis 2–3 is often used as evidence: Adam and Eve were able to choose whether to obey God's command, and their choice to disobey shows humans bear genuine moral responsibility.\n• The free will defence (used in response to the problem of evil) argues that moral evil exists because people misuse their freedom, not because God directly wills evil.\nDeterminism and predestination\n• Determinism is the view that all events, including human choices, are caused by prior conditions and so could not have happened otherwise.\n• Predestination, especially associated with the reformer John Calvin, teaches that God has already decided, before the beginning of time, who will be saved (the \"elect\"); Romans 8:29–30 is often cited, describing those God \"foreknew he also predestined\".\n• Double predestination goes further, holding that God has also predetermined who will be damned — a controversial and minority position even within Calvinist traditions.\nResponses\n• Arminian and Catholic theology generally reject strict predestination, emphasising that God's grace is offered to all and that individuals freely respond to it.\n• Compatibilists argue that divine foreknowledge (God knowing what will happen) does not necessarily mean God causes it to happen, allowing genuine human freedom and God's omniscience to coexist.\nExam link: be ready to explain both free will and predestination as genuine Christian positions, and to show how they connect to the problem of evil and moral responsibility."},{"id":"c-p1-1.5","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.5","title":"Ultimate reality","specFocus":"Christian belief about the nature and character of God, including the Trinity, and why views of God's nature vary.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.5 Ultimate Reality\nChristianity is monotheistic: Christians believe in one God, who is the ultimate reality underlying and sustaining everything that exists.\nCharacteristics of God\n• Omnipotent: all-powerful, able to do anything that is logically possible.\n• Omniscient: all-knowing, aware of everything past, present and future.\n• Omnibenevolent: perfectly good and loving; 1 John 4:8 states simply, \"God is love\".\n• Eternal and transcendent, yet also immanent (present and active within creation and in believers' lives).\nThe Trinity\n• Christians believe God exists as a Trinity — one God in three persons: Father, Son (Jesus Christ) and Holy Spirit — a central but mysterious doctrine that distinguishes Christianity from other monotheistic faiths.\n• The Trinity is not three separate gods, but three persons sharing one divine nature, often illustrated (imperfectly) using analogies such as water existing as ice, liquid and steam.\nDifferent attitudes to God's nature\n• Catholic and Orthodox Christians often approach God through rich tradition, sacraments and the intercession of saints and Mary, emphasising God's majesty and mystery.\n• Many Protestant Christians emphasise a direct, personal relationship with God through Jesus, without the same role for saints or Mary as intermediaries.\n• Classical theism stresses God's unchanging power and perfection; process theology (a minority, more modern view) suggests God's power works more through persuasion and relationship than total control, particularly in response to the problem of suffering.\nExam link: \"characteristics of God\" questions want named attributes (omnipotent, omniscient, loving, Trinity); \"different attitudes\" questions want a reason for the difference, such as denominational tradition."},{"id":"c-p1-1.6","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.6","title":"Belief, uncertainty and unbelief","specFocus":"Reasons for and against belief in God's existence, including key arguments and the challenge of unfalsifiability.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.6 Belief, Uncertainty and Unbelief\nThis module looks at why some people believe in God, why others doubt or reject belief, and how confident any of these positions can be.\nReasons for belief\n• The design (teleological) argument: William Paley compared the complexity of the natural world to a watch, arguing that just as a watch implies a watchmaker, the ordered complexity of the universe implies an intelligent designer.\n• The cosmological argument: everything that exists has a cause; an infinite regress of causes is considered impossible, so there must be a first, uncaused cause — God.\n• Religious experience: a personal sense of God's presence, answered prayer, or a transformative encounter, which believers interpret as direct evidence of God.\n• Upbringing and revelation: many people come to believe through family, church community, and trust in the Bible as God's revealed word.\nReasons for doubt or unbelief\n• The problem of evil and suffering: if God is all-powerful and all-loving, why does suffering exist? Some see this as strong evidence against God's existence.\n• Lack of empirical proof: scientific explanations for the origin of the universe (Big Bang) and life (evolution) do not require God as a cause.\n• Antony Flew's falsification challenge argues that if no evidence could ever count against belief in God, then claims about God may not be meaningful, testable claims at all.\n• Agnosticism holds that it is simply impossible to know for certain whether God exists, while atheism positively denies God's existence.\nExam link: \"Evaluate\" questions on this topic reward balancing specific arguments (design, cosmological, problem of evil, falsification) rather than vague assertions that \"some people believe and some don't\"."},{"id":"c-p1-1.7","paper":"Paper 1","module":"1.7","title":"The problem of evil and suffering","specFocus":"The problem of evil as a challenge to belief in God, and Christian theodicies that try to respond to it.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.7 The Problem of Evil and Suffering\nThe problem of evil is one of the most serious philosophical challenges to Christian belief: if God is omnipotent and omnibenevolent, why does evil and suffering exist?\nThe logical problem\n• Epicurus, and later J. L. Mackie, expressed this as an \"inconsistent triad\": an omnipotent, omnibenevolent God should be both able and willing to prevent evil; since evil exists, such a God, it is argued, cannot exist.\n• Moral evil (suffering caused by human choices, e.g. cruelty, war) is often distinguished from natural evil (suffering from natural causes, e.g. disease, earthquakes), since they require different kinds of explanation.\nChristian responses (theodicies)\n• Augustinian theodicy: evil entered a perfect creation through the Fall (Genesis 3); evil is not a \"thing\" God created but a privation — a corruption or absence of the good — resulting from humanity's misuse of free will.\n• Free will defence: moral evil results from God's gift of genuine human freedom; a world with real freedom (and therefore the possibility of its misuse) is considered more valuable than a world of forced, puppet-like obedience.\n• Irenaean/soul-making theodicy (developed by John Hick): the world is a deliberately imperfect \"vale of soul-making\" in which facing real challenges and suffering allows humans to develop moral virtues such as courage, compassion and perseverance.\n• The Book of Job presents a case where a righteous man suffers without clear explanation, suggesting that some suffering may serve a purpose beyond human understanding, rather than disproving God.\nExam link: \"Evaluate\" answers should set theodicies (Augustinian, Irenaean, free will) directly against the inconsistent triad, rather than simply describing each theory in isolation."},{"id":"c-p1-2.1","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.1","title":"Death and life after death","specFocus":"Christian beliefs about death, resurrection, judgement, heaven and hell, contrasted with non-religious views.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.1 Death and Life After Death\nChristian belief about death centres on the resurrection of Jesus, which is treated as both proof and guarantee of life beyond physical death for believers.\nResurrection and judgement\n• 1 Corinthians 15:20 calls Jesus \"the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep\", meaning his resurrection is the first example of a resurrection all believers hope to share.\n• Most Christians believe in a final judgement, at which each person's life is assessed by God, leading to eternal life in heaven (full relationship with God) or separation from God, often called hell.\n• Catholic teaching additionally includes purgatory, a state of purification after death for those who die in God's friendship but are not yet fully ready for heaven; most Protestants reject purgatory, emphasising judgement and salvation through faith at the point of death.\nWhy believe in life after death?\n• John 3:16 promises that \"whoever believes in him shall not perish but have eternal life\" — a central motivation for Christian hope.\n• Belief in ultimate justice: if good people suffer unfairly and wrongdoers sometimes prosper in this life, an afterlife allows for final justice.\nNon-religious views\n• Humanists argue personality and consciousness depend entirely on the brain, so death is the permanent end of a person's existence; this life is the only one we have, giving strong reason to make it meaningful now.\nExam link: distinguish clearly between \"non-religious reasons to believe in an afterlife\" (e.g. near-death experiences) and \"non-religious reasons to reject an afterlife\" (e.g. no evidence of consciousness without a brain) — questions can ask for either."},{"id":"c-p1-2.2","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.2","title":"The meaning and purpose of life","specFocus":"Christian and non-religious views on what gives life meaning and purpose.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.2 The Meaning and Purpose of Life\nChristians and non-religious people often answer the question \"what is life for?\" very differently.\nChristian teaching\n• The Westminster Shorter Catechism summarises Christian purpose succinctly: \"man's chief end is to glorify God, and to enjoy him forever.\"\n• Jesus taught that the greatest commandments are to love God fully and to love one's neighbour as oneself (Mark 12:30–31); for Christians, purpose is found in relationship with God and in service to others.\n• Building the \"Kingdom of God\" — working for justice, peace and love in the world — is seen by many Christians as part of their life's purpose, following Jesus' example and teaching.\nNon-religious views\n• Humanists find meaning through relationships, creativity, learning, contributing to society and helping others flourish, without appeal to God or an afterlife.\n• Because this life is believed to be the only one, Humanists argue it should be treated as uniquely precious, giving strong motivation to live well and ethically now.\nComparing the views\n• Both views can lead to similar ethical behaviour (kindness, honesty, service) but differ on the ultimate source and permanence of meaning — eternal relationship with God versus value created within a single finite life.\nExam link: \"Evaluate\" questions on this topic reward showing you understand both sources of meaning can motivate genuinely good lives, rather than assuming only religious people can live meaningfully."},{"id":"c-p1-2.3","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.3","title":"Sanctity of life, abortion and euthanasia","specFocus":"Christian teaching on the value of human life and differing Christian and non-religious attitudes to abortion and euthanasia.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.3 Sanctity of Life, Abortion and Euthanasia\nSanctity of life is the belief that human life is sacred and has inherent value because it is a gift from God, which shapes Christian views on abortion and euthanasia.\nSanctity of life\n• Genesis 1:27: humans are made \"in the image of God\" (imago Dei), giving every person inherent worth regardless of ability, age or health.\n• Exodus 20:13, \"You shall not murder\", is widely used to argue that only God has authority over when human life begins and ends.\nAbortion\n• Most Christian denominations, especially the Catholic Church, teach that life begins at conception, so abortion is viewed as ending an innocent human life and is generally opposed, except in rare cases to save the mother's life.\n• Some more liberal Christians weigh the sanctity of the foetus's life against compassion for the mother's circumstances, arguing that abortion may sometimes be, in Joseph Fletcher's situation-ethics terms, \"the most loving thing to do\".\n• Non-religious views on abortion often emphasise the woman's right to choose (bodily autonomy) over the moral status of the foetus.\nEuthanasia\n• Most Christians oppose euthanasia, citing Job 1:21 (\"the Lord gave, and the Lord has taken away\") to argue that only God should decide when life ends.\n• The hospice movement, pioneered by the Christian Dame Cicely Saunders, is often presented by Christians as a compassionate alternative that manages pain and suffering without ending life.\n• Supporters of legal euthanasia emphasise personal autonomy and compassion for unbearable suffering, especially in terminal illness.\nExam link: keep abortion and euthanasia arguments separate even though both relate to sanctity of life — questions will usually specify which issue to address."},{"id":"c-p1-2.4","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.4","title":"Human relationships","specFocus":"Christian teaching on sexual relationships, and the range of Christian attitudes to same-sex relationships.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.4 Human Relationships\nChristian teaching generally links sexual relationships closely to marriage, though attitudes to specific relationships vary between denominations.\nHeterosexual relationships\n• Genesis 2:24 describes marriage as the context in which \"a man shall leave his father and his mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh\" — many Christians see marriage as the intended setting for sexual intimacy.\n• Sex within marriage is traditionally understood to serve both unity (strengthening the couple's bond) and procreation (having children), though views on strictness around sex outside marriage vary between conservative and more liberal Christians.\nSame-sex relationships\n• Traditionalist Christians cite passages such as Leviticus 18:22 and Romans 1:26–27 as evidence that the Bible condemns same-sex sexual acts, and so teach that marriage should remain between a man and a woman.\n• More inclusive Christian traditions (e.g. Quakers, the United Reformed Church, and parts of the Anglican Communion) emphasise Jesus' teaching to love one's neighbour and the equal worth of all people made in God's image, and have moved towards blessing or permitting same-sex marriages.\n• Differences often come down to how each tradition interprets the Bible: whether certain Old Testament laws are seen as timeless commands or as culturally bound and no longer directly binding.\nExam link: when discussing \"different attitudes\", name specific denominations or traditions (e.g. Catholic vs Quaker) rather than generalising about \"some Christians\" without any detail."},{"id":"c-p1-2.5","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.5","title":"Marriage and Partnership","specFocus":"Why marriage matters to Christians, and Christian attitudes to cohabitation and sex outside marriage.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.5 Marriage and Partnership\nFor most Christians, marriage is far more than a legal contract — it is a sacred covenant made before God.\nWhy marriage matters\n• Marriage is often understood as mirroring the relationship between Christ and the Church: Ephesians 5:25 instructs, \"husbands, love your wives, as Christ loved the church.\"\n• For Catholics, marriage is one of the seven sacraments, believed to convey God's grace to the couple and making the marriage vows sacred and, in Catholic teaching, indissoluble.\n• Marriage provides, for most Christians, the proper context for sexual intimacy and for bringing up children within a stable, committed relationship.\nCohabitation and sex outside marriage\n• More conservative Christians oppose cohabitation and sex outside marriage, seeing marriage vows as essential before sexual intimacy is appropriate.\n• Many Christians today, reflecting wider social change, are more accepting of couples living together before or instead of marriage, especially where the relationship is loving and committed.\n• Non-religious people generally see no inherent problem with cohabitation, judging relationships by mutual commitment and care rather than by legal or religious marriage status.\nCivil partnerships\n• Civil partnerships and civil marriage give same-sex (and opposite-sex) couples legal recognition and rights without necessarily implying a religious ceremony, which some churches support and others do not formally recognise.\nExam link: \"Explain why marriage is important\" answers should connect to specific teaching (Ephesians 5:25, sacrament, covenant) rather than just asserting marriage is \"important to Christians\"."},{"id":"c-p1-2.6","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.6","title":"Divorce and Remarriage","specFocus":"Jesus' teaching on divorce, and how Catholic and Protestant attitudes to divorce and remarriage differ.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.6 Divorce and Remarriage\nJesus' own teaching on marriage's permanence creates an important tension for Christians facing divorce and remarriage.\nJesus' teaching on divorce\n• Mark 10:2–9 records Jesus teaching that marriage is intended by God to be permanent, concluding: \"what God has joined together, let no one separate.\"\n• Matthew 19:9 includes an exception clause permitting divorce in the case of sexual immorality (porneia), which different denominations interpret more or less strictly.\nDenominational differences on divorce\n• The Catholic Church generally does not recognise divorce; a Catholic marriage is considered indissoluble, so the Church instead offers annulment, a declaration that a valid sacramental marriage never actually existed (for example due to a serious defect in consent).\n• Many Protestant churches permit divorce, particularly in cases of adultery, abuse or irretrievable breakdown, emphasising God's forgiveness and the pastoral reality of human failure.\nRemarriage\n• The Catholic Church typically will not remarry a divorced person in church while a former spouse is alive, unless an annulment has been granted.\n• The Church of England changed its policy in 2002, allowing remarriage of divorcees in church at the discretion of the local vicar — a significant pastoral shift balancing the ideal of lifelong marriage with compassion for those who have experienced breakdown.\n• Most other Protestant churches are generally willing to bless remarriage, stressing grace, forgiveness and the possibility of a fresh start.\nExam link: \"Choose one religion\" questions on remarriage reward naming a specific denominational policy (e.g. Catholic annulment, Church of England's 2002 change) rather than a vague \"Christians have mixed views\"."},{"id":"c-p1-2.7","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.7","title":"Family Structures and Responsibilities","specFocus":"Why family life matters to Christians, and debates about religious upbringing and family responsibilities.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.7 Family Structures and Responsibilities\nFamily is seen by most Christians as the primary place where faith, values and love are first taught and experienced.\nPassing on faith\n• Deuteronomy 6:6–7 instructs parents to teach God's commandments diligently to their children, \"when you sit in your house, and when you walk by the way\" — showing the family's central role in religious education long before formal church teaching.\n• Ephesians 5–6 sets out household codes describing mutual duties within families: love between spouses, children honouring parents, and parents raising children \"in the discipline and instruction of the Lord\" (Ephesians 6:4).\n• Infant baptism, godparents and family worship are practical ways Christian families commit, from a child's earliest life, to nurturing their faith.\nFamily structures and roles\n• Christian views on gender roles within the family vary: some more traditional Christians see distinct, complementary roles for men and women; many other Christians support full equality between spouses in family decision-making and responsibilities.\n• Family responsibility also extends to caring for elderly or vulnerable relatives, which many Christians see as an extension of the commandment to honour one's parents (Exodus 20:12).\nDebates about religious upbringing\n• Many Christians see bringing children up in the faith as a loving duty that gives children identity, moral grounding and community.\n• Humanists and some liberal thinkers argue children should be free to explore beliefs and decide for themselves once old enough, cautioning against indoctrination.\nExam link: \"Evaluate\" answers on religious upbringing should weigh parental duty/community support against concerns about a child's freedom to choose — not just assert one side."},{"id":"c-p1-2.8","paper":"Paper 1","module":"2.8","title":"Childlessness and Celibacy","specFocus":"Christian attitudes to celibacy, contraception and childlessness, including why celibacy is valued in some traditions.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.8 Childlessness and Celibacy\nChristian teaching recognises a range of situations around children and sexual relationships, including the deliberate choice of celibacy.\nCelibacy\n• St Paul, in 1 Corinthians 7:32–35, teaches that an unmarried person can be \"anxious about the things of the Lord\" without the competing responsibilities of marriage — meaning celibacy is valued as a way to devote oneself fully to God's service.\n• In Catholic and Orthodox tradition, priests, monks and nuns are generally required to remain celibate, following what is understood as the model of Jesus' own unmarried life and symbolising total dedication to the Church.\n• Most Protestant traditions do not require clerical celibacy, allowing ministers to marry and have families.\nChildlessness\n• Couples may be childless involuntarily (infertility) or by choice; Christian attitudes to assisted fertility treatment vary, with the Catholic Church generally opposing techniques that involve destroying embryos or separating conception from the marital act, while many Protestant Christians are more accepting of IVF and similar treatments.\n• Adoption and fostering are widely encouraged by Christian churches as a loving response to childlessness and a way of caring for children in need of a family.\nContraception\n• The Catholic Church teaches that artificial contraception is not permitted, as it separates the unitive and procreative purposes of marriage, though natural family planning is accepted.\n• Most Protestant Christians accept contraception as a responsible way for couples to plan their family.\nExam link: do not confuse celibacy (a deliberate vocation, often linked to ministry) with childlessness (which may be involuntary) — questions distinguish clearly between the two."},{"id":"c-p2-1.1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.1","title":"Jesus: the incarnate Son of God","specFocus":"The doctrine of the incarnation and its significance for Christian belief about Jesus.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.1 Jesus: The Incarnate Son of God\nThe incarnation is the belief that God the Son became fully human in Jesus Christ while remaining fully divine — \"the Word became flesh and dwelt among us\" (John 1:14).\nKey beliefs\n• The virgin birth: Luke 1:26–35 describes Jesus being conceived by the Holy Spirit and born to Mary, showing he was divine from the very start of his human life.\n• Fully God and fully human: the Nicene Creed states Jesus is \"true God from true God\", while Hebrews 4:15 stresses he was tempted \"as we are, yet without sin\", meaning he can fully understand human experience.\nWhy the incarnation matters\n• It demonstrates the depth of God's love: John 3:16, \"God so loved the world that he gave his only Son.\"\n• It underpins Christian belief in salvation: Jesus' death is understood as an atoning sacrifice that could only reconcile humanity with God because he was both truly human (representing humanity) and truly divine (with the power to defeat sin and death).\n• It gives Christians a model of how to live — Jesus' life and teaching provide a direct example of God's character expressed in human form.\nExam link: keep the incarnation (Jesus becoming human) distinct from the resurrection (Jesus rising from death) — they are different, though connected, beliefs, and questions may ask about either specifically."},{"id":"c-p2-1.2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.2","title":"The Bible and its authority","specFocus":"Why the Bible matters to Christians and why Christians interpret its authority differently.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.2 The Bible and Its Authority\nThe Bible is the foundational sacred text of Christianity, containing the Old Testament (shared with Judaism) and the New Testament, including the Gospels describing Jesus' life.\nWhy the Bible matters\n• 2 Timothy 3:16 states \"all Scripture is breathed out by God\", meaning Christians see it as divinely inspired, not just a human historical document.\n• The Gospels record Jesus' life, teaching, death and resurrection, giving Christians their central story and moral example.\n• The Bible is used in worship, prayer, teaching and personal devotion, shaping both belief and daily conduct.\nDifferent views of authority\n• Biblical literalists/fundamentalists believe the Bible is inerrant (without error) and should be followed exactly as written, including, for some, a literal six-day creation.\n• Liberal Christians see the Bible as written by inspired human authors within a particular historical and cultural context, meaning some passages are read symbolically or reinterpreted for today (e.g. many accept evolution alongside Genesis as a theological, not scientific, text).\n• Catholics hold that Church tradition and teaching authority (the Magisterium) work alongside scripture in guiding belief; many Protestants hold to \"sola scriptura\" — scripture alone as the final authority.\nExam link: \"Discuss whether the Bible should be read literally\" answers should explicitly use the terms literalism, inerrancy and sola scriptura/Magisterium to show precise understanding."},{"id":"c-p2-1.3","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.3","title":"Leadership in the Church","specFocus":"Different structures and forms of ordained and lay leadership across Christian denominations.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.3 Leadership in the Church\nChristian denominations organise leadership differently, reflecting different beliefs about authority and tradition.\nOrdained ministry\n• Ordination, often through the \"laying on of hands\", formally sets a person apart to lead worship, preach, teach and administer sacraments such as the Eucharist.\n• Catholic and Orthodox churches maintain a hierarchy of deacons, priests and bishops, with the Pope leading the worldwide Catholic Church; this reflects belief in apostolic succession — unbroken leadership passed down from Jesus' original apostles.\n• Many Protestant churches have simpler structures, with ministers elected or appointed by the local congregation, reflecting the Reformation-era idea of the \"priesthood of all believers\" — that every Christian has direct access to God.\nDebates in leadership\n• Ordination of women: many Anglican and Protestant denominations now ordain women as priests or ministers; the Catholic and Orthodox churches ordain only men, citing tradition and their interpretation of scripture.\n• Lay leadership: many churches rely heavily on lay (non-ordained) people for prayer groups, Sunday school and pastoral care, showing leadership is not only exercised by the ordained.\nExam link: \"Discuss\" questions about leadership reward comparing ordained authority (sacraments, apostolic succession) against lay/congregational involvement (priesthood of all believers), not simply describing one church's structure."},{"id":"c-p2-1.4","paper":"Paper 2","module":"1.4","title":"One other significant Christian","specFocus":"Martin Luther King Jr as a worked example of a significant Christian figure whose faith shaped social action.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"1.4 One Other Significant Christian — Martin Luther King Jr\nMartin Luther King Jr (1929–1968) was a Baptist minister and the most prominent leader of the American Civil Rights Movement, whose Christian faith directly shaped his activism.\nKey actions\n• He led the Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955–56), a non-violent protest against racial segregation on public buses, sparked by Rosa Parks' arrest.\n• He led the March on Washington (1963), delivering his famous \"I Have a Dream\" speech, drawing on biblical imagery of justice and equality.\n• He wrote the \"Letter from Birmingham Jail\" (1963), defending non-violent civil disobedience using Christian moral reasoning.\n• He was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize (1964) for his leadership, and was assassinated in 1968.\nWhy he is significant for Christians\n• His commitment to non-violence was rooted in Jesus' teaching to \"turn the other cheek\" (Matthew 5:39) and to love one's enemies.\n• His campaign for racial justice is seen by many as working for the \"Kingdom of God\" on earth, applying belief in imago Dei (that all people are made equally in God's image) to a major social injustice.\n• His willingness to suffer and ultimately die for his beliefs is often compared to Christian ideas of sacrificial discipleship.\nExam link: be ready to compare King with other possible significant Christians (e.g. Mother Teresa, Pope John Paul II) when asked to discuss \"the most significant Christian\"."},{"id":"c-p2-2.1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"2.1","title":"Christmas and Easter","specFocus":"The meaning and celebration of the two most important festivals in the Christian calendar.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.1 Christmas and Easter\nChristmas and Easter are the two most widely recognised Christian festivals, celebrating the incarnation and the resurrection respectively.\nChristmas\n• Celebrates the birth of Jesus, the incarnation of God as a human being, central to the Christmas story in Luke 2 and Matthew 1–2.\n• Celebrated through Advent (a season of preparation), nativity plays, carol services and, for many, Midnight Mass on Christmas Eve.\nEaster\n• Celebrates Jesus' resurrection from the dead, which St Paul calls the very foundation of Christian faith (1 Corinthians 15:14).\n• Preceded by Lent (40 days of fasting, prayer and reflection) and Holy Week — Maundy Thursday (the Last Supper), Good Friday (the crucifixion) and Holy Saturday — before Easter Sunday celebrations, often including the lighting of the Paschal (Easter) candle and the greeting \"Christ is risen! He is risen indeed!\"\nWhich is more important?\n• Theologically, many Christians consider Easter the more significant festival, since the resurrection, not the birth narrative alone, is presented in the New Testament as proof that Jesus overcame sin and death.\n• Christmas, however, remains hugely popular and widely celebrated, though often criticised by Christians for becoming commercialised and losing its religious focus in wider secular culture.\nExam link: \"Discuss\" questions often ask you to weigh the cultural/commercial side of Christmas against its continuing religious meaning for practising Christians — use both sides."},{"id":"c-p2-2.2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"2.2","title":"Pilgrimage in Christianity","specFocus":"Why Christians undertake pilgrimage, using Lourdes and Jerusalem as key examples.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"2.2 Pilgrimage in Christianity\nPilgrimage is a journey to a place of religious significance, undertaken to deepen faith, seek healing, or connect physically with events central to Christian belief.\nLourdes\n• A Catholic pilgrimage site in France, where Bernadette Soubirous reported visions of the Virgin Mary in 1858.\n• Pilgrims visit the grotto where the apparitions are said to have occurred, and bathe in or drink water from a spring believed by many to have healing properties.\n• The sick and disabled are specially welcomed and assisted by volunteers, making Lourdes strongly associated with healing, hope and compassion.\nJerusalem\n• Contains sites directly connected to Jesus' final days, including the Via Dolorosa (the traditional route to his crucifixion) and the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, built on the site traditionally believed to be both Golgotha (the crucifixion site) and Jesus' tomb.\n• Pilgrimage to Jerusalem, especially during Holy Week, allows Christians to feel a direct, physical connection to events described in the Gospels.\nIs pilgrimage essential?\n• Supporters say it deepens faith, builds community and can provide profound spiritual experiences.\n• Critics note it is not required by core doctrine or the sacraments, can be costly and physically demanding, and that God can be encountered anywhere, not only at holy sites.\nExam link: name specific sites (Lourdes, Jerusalem) and specific features (grotto, healing water, Via Dolorosa, Holy Sepulchre) rather than describing pilgrimage only in general terms."},{"id":"c-p2-3.1","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.1","title":"Churches: design and features","specFocus":"How and why church buildings are designed differently across Christian traditions.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"3.1 Churches: Design and Features\nChurch buildings reflect the beliefs and worship styles of different Christian traditions.\nCatholic churches\n• The altar is the focal point, where the priest celebrates the Eucharist.\n• Statues or images of Mary and the saints, the Stations of the Cross (depicting Jesus' crucifixion), and the tabernacle (where the consecrated bread is reserved) are common features, supporting prayer, reflection and teaching.\nProtestant churches\n• Often plainer in design, reflecting the Reformation's emphasis on scripture and preaching over visual imagery; the pulpit (for preaching) is frequently more prominent than the altar/communion table.\n• Quaker meeting houses are especially simple, with seating arranged so worshippers face one another, supporting their tradition of silent worship without set liturgy.\nWhy designs differ\n• Catholic and Orthodox traditions see beauty, icons and imagery as aids to worship and teaching.\n• Protestant traditions generally prioritise the spoken and read word of God over visual decoration.\n• Layout reflects worship style: altar-facing naves suit liturgical, sacrament-centred worship; circular or facing seating suits more participatory or silent worship.\nAre buildings necessary?\n• They provide a dedicated space and house items needed for sacraments (altar, font), but the early Church met in homes, and Christians today still worship in prisons, homes and the open air where buildings are unavailable.\nExam link: match specific features (altar, pulpit, stations of the cross, plain meeting house) to the denomination that uses them when answering \"Outline\" questions."},{"id":"c-p2-3.2","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.2","title":"Christian worship, prayer and the Eucharist","specFocus":"The meaning of the Eucharist, the role of prayer, and the balance between private and public worship.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"3.2 Christian Worship, Prayer and the Eucharist\nWorship and prayer are central, regular practices through which Christians express and deepen their relationship with God.\nThe Eucharist\n• Re-enacts the Last Supper, where Jesus shared bread and wine with his disciples, saying \"this is my body\" and \"this is my blood\" (Mark 14:22–24).\n• Catholics believe in transubstantiation — that the bread and wine actually become the body and blood of Christ while retaining their appearance.\n• Many Protestants see the bread and wine as a symbolic or spiritual act of remembrance rather than a literal transformation.\nPrayer\n• Jesus taught the Lord's Prayer (Matthew 6:9–13) as a model for how Christians should pray.\n• Prayer often includes praise, confession, thanksgiving and requests (sometimes summarised as ACTS), and can be offered alone or with others.\nPrivate and public worship\n• Private worship (personal prayer, Bible reading) deepens an individual's relationship with God and follows Jesus' own example of withdrawing to pray alone (Luke 5:16).\n• Public worship brings Christians together as the \"body of Christ\" (1 Corinthians 12), enabling shared sacraments and mutual encouragement; Hebrews 10:25 instructs believers not to neglect \"meeting together\".\nExam link: \"Discuss\" questions on worship reward weighing private devotion against the scriptural importance of gathering, rather than treating the two as simply equal without comparison."},{"id":"c-p2-3.3","paper":"Paper 2","module":"3.3","title":"Rites of passage in Christian life","specFocus":"Baptism, confirmation and other key rites of passage that mark stages of Christian life.","sourcePages":"Written to match the Edexcel specification; no supplied booklet for this subject.","summary":"3.3 Rites of Passage in Christian Life\nRites of passage mark important transitions in a Christian's life and relationship with the Church.\nBaptism\n• Infant baptism (common in Catholic, Orthodox and many Protestant churches): a baby is baptised with water, often poured over the head, while godparents and parents make promises on the child's behalf to raise them in the Christian faith.\n• Believer's baptism (practised by Baptists and some others): a person old enough to personally profess faith is fully immersed in water, symbolising dying to an old life and rising to new life in Christ (Romans 6:4).\n• The two approaches reflect a genuine denominational disagreement: whether baptism should respond to personal, conscious faith, or whether it can express God's grace reaching a child before they can respond, similar to circumcision in the Old Testament covenant.\nConfirmation\n• Allows someone baptised as an infant to personally confirm the promises made on their behalf, taking on the faith as their own choice.\n• In Catholic and Orthodox traditions, a bishop lays hands on and anoints the candidate with chrism oil, understood to strengthen them with the Holy Spirit (echoing Pentecost, Acts 2).\n• Often marks full, mature membership of the Church, including full participation in receiving the Eucharist.\nOther rites of passage\n• Marriage and funeral services are also key Christian rites of passage, marking the formation of a new family and the committal of the deceased to God's care with the hope of resurrection.\nExam link: when asked to \"Discuss whether baptism should only be performed on believing adults\", name the Baptist position explicitly and contrast it with infant baptism traditions, rather than discussing baptism in general."}],"rubrics":{"Paper 1":{"3":"Identify (3 marks): award 1 mark for each valid factual point, up to 3. No development is required for the point to receive its identification mark. Accept other valid responses; do not credit repetition.","4":"Outline (4 marks): normally two distinct points, each with a relevant description (1) and development (1). Development must relate to the point and the question; do not award repeated development.","6":"Explain (6 marks): AO1 knowledge and understanding = 3 marks; AO2 explanation/analysis of significance and influence = 3 marks. Best-fit levels: 1–2 limited/superficial; 3–4 mostly accurate and developed; 5–6 accurate, comprehensive understanding with well-developed explanation and judgements.","12":"Evaluate (12 marks): AO1 knowledge and understanding = 4 marks; AO2 analysis/evaluation = 8 marks. Level 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge, superficial analysis, asserted judgements. Level 2 (4–6): limited understanding, some developed arguments, simple judgements and limited justification. Level 3 (7–9): accurate understanding, logical chains of reasoning across different views, reasoned judgements and a partially justified conclusion. Level 4 (10–12): sustained, accurate and thorough knowledge; coherent logical analysis of different views; appraisal of arguments; justified conclusion consistent with the analysis."},"Paper 2":{"4":"Outline (4 marks): normally two relevant points, each with a description (1) and development (1). Development must relate to the point and question. Credit any other valid Christian response; do not reward repetition or unrelated development.","6":"Explain (6 marks): AO1 knowledge and understanding = 3 marks; AO2 analysis of significance/influence = 3 marks. Best-fit levels: 1–2 limited/superficial; 3–4 mostly accurate and developed; 5–6 accurate, comprehensive understanding with well-developed explanation and judgements.","10":"Discuss (10 marks): AO1 knowledge and understanding = 4 marks; AO2 analysis = 6 marks. Level 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge and superficial analysis with little consideration of how aspects interrelate. Level 2 (4–6): limited understanding and basic analysis with some developed arguments but limited interrelation. Level 3 (7–8): accurate understanding and logical reasoning considering different aspects and how they interrelate. Level 4 (9–10): sustained accurate and thorough knowledge; coherent logical reasoning considering different aspects and their interrelationships in a sustained way."}},"moduleCounts":{"paper1":15,"paper2":9},"meta":{"title":"Edexcel IGCSE Religious Studies — Christianity","qualification":"International GCSE Religious Studies, specification for assessment from 2027","questionTotal":57,"paper1QuestionCount":30,"paper2QuestionCount":27,"modulesTotal":24,"provenance":"Questions are original practice items written to match the official Edexcel International GCSE Religious Studies (4RS1) specification structure. They are NOT transcribed from an official question booklet or past paper — unlike the app's Hinduism pack, no official booklet was supplied for this subject. Model answers are indicative exemplars only, not official Edexcel mark schemes."}});
